4.  CHANNEL -- per row, the mutant it kills and the observable that differs
====================================================================================

  ROW                        KILLS                    OBSERVABLE THAT DIFFERS
  ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  M / t7  (1st byte back)    E2: swap the flag        the push is refused, so the ring
                             operand for              is empty and the pop returns the
                             `d->atapi_st != NULL`    -1 sentinel:  5a  ->  ff
  M / v0  (2nd byte back)    E2, same mechanism       a5  ->  ff.  The SECOND read also
                                                      pins that the -1 return survives
                                                      the burned latch (see block 1)
  M / t7 + M / v0            M1: drop the flag        after the reset atapi_phase == 0
                             conjunct, leaving        and PHASE_CMDOUT is nonzero, so
                             `phase != PHASE_CMDOUT`  the residual term still refuses:
                                                      5a,a5 -> ff,ff
  N / v1  +  N / a0          E3: `&& d->cur_command    after SOFT_RESET cur_command is
  (the SECOND no-fill        != WDCC_RECAL` bolted    0x08, the exemption does not
   vehicle)                  onto the swapped guard   apply, the writes are refused:
                                                      5a,a5 -> ff,ff.  **The M rows
                                                      stay GREEN under E3** -- that is
                                                      the point of a second vehicle
  N / v1  +  N / a0          E2 and M1 also           same mechanism as the M rows; the
                                                      N arm is not vehicle-specific

  *** THE SECOND ROW HAS A KILL OF ITS OWN, NAMED BY THE fable5 SEAT AND MISSING FROM
  THE FIRST DRAFT OF THIS BLOCK. ***  Written as above, M/v0 looked like belt-and-braces
  on M/t7.  It is not.  A mutant admitting a push only into an EMPTY ring -- an
  `inbuf_head == inbuf_tail` gate, drawn from BODY-read fields and therefore squarely
  inside the DEPENDS-ON enumeration -- PASSES the first row (ring empty, push lands,
  t7 = 5a) and fails ONLY the second (ring now holds one byte, push refused, v0 = ff).
  **The first row is structurally blind to that whole family.**  So the two rows kill
  different things and neither is redundant.

VACUOUS-BY-CONSTRUCTION CHECK: both rows name a channel that CAN vary under their named
mutants.

*** THE ESCAPE FIGURE IS RE-MEASURED, NOT CARRIED. ***  The `22/22 ESCAPED` above came
from the pass-2 seat's scratch run, and the fable5 seat established that its cited
artifact (`opus495/wave2/run_q3.out`) NO LONGER EXISTS, while the ledger's witness field
says `20/20` and its own note says `all 22 rows` -- a number disagreeing with itself and
with its evidence.  **This table therefore runs E2 against the COMMITTED (pre-extension)
probe as its own leg**, so the escape is a measurement of this round rather than a
transcription of a vanished one.  That is the frozen-number class caught one generation
earlier than usual: before the number entered the record, not after.  The channel is the data port's own readback, which is the same channel rows B
and G/H/I already prove is device-served and FIFO-ordered rather than RAM.

PREDICTED SCORES, WRITTEN BEFORE THE TABLE RUNS so a fitted number is detectable:

FOUR accept rows now, not two -- the N arm was added on pass 1 (see the amendment).
Each row contributes TWO predictions, one per leg, so the detector is 15 rows / 30.

    base (no mutant)   30 / 30,  0 red
    E2                 26 / 30,  4 red   was 22/22 ESCAPED  <- the round's whole purpose
    E3  (cur_command   28 / 30,  2 red   the M rows stay GREEN; only N reds.  A mutant
         exemption)                      NOTHING in the detector could see before today
    M1                 24 / 30,  6 red   was 20/22 (2 red), plus 4 new
    M2 M3 M4 M5 M6      old numerator + 8, out of 30   (all four accept rows green)
    M7                 15 / 30, 15 red -- THE ACCEPT ARMS NEVER RUN.  See below.

  A row GREEN under a mutant scores on BOTH legs (+2); a row RED scores on the shipped
  leg only (+1, 1 red).  That is where the 4-red and 2-red figures come from, and it is
  the same arithmetic TABLE.txt's 20/22 for M1 already follows.

  Reasoning for the five, stated so it can be refuted: after the reset the flag is 0, and
  each either still requires the flag (M3 and M6 keep it as a conjunct), or is inert once
  the guard is not taken (M2 needs PHASE_COMPLETED, M4 removes the guard), or keys on a
  phase the reset has left at 0 (M5 tests `== PHASE_DATAIN`).

  *** AND THE SIXTH PREDICTION WAS REFUTED BY A SEAT BEFORE THE TABLE RAN, WHICH IS
  EXACTLY WHAT WRITING PREDICTIONS DOWN IS FOR. ***  The first draft of this block put M7
  with the greens on the same reasoning -- "it keeps the flag as a conjunct, so after
  RECAL the guard is not taken."  **That reasoning is sound and the conclusion is still
  wrong, because it assumes the M arm executes at all.**  M7 replaces the refusal body
  with `return 0;`, and the guard DOES fire on the DATA-IN injection at row E -- flag 1,
  phase DATAIN.  The handler returns 0 there, the mutant guest takes EXCEPTION_DBE and
  never parks, which is #495's own recorded result for M7.  So the guest is dead long
  before the M arm is reached: `t7` and `v0` come back `??`, both new rows red on the
  mutant leg, and the score is 13 of 26 with 13 red rather than a green-M control.

  >  **M7 IS ALREADY KILLED AND MUST NOT BE CITED AS EVIDENCE THAT ROW M IS INERT UNDER
  >  IT.**  The row is not inert; it is unreached.  Those are different facts and only
  >  one of them is a control.  Found by the grok seat, reading #495's own M7 record.
  >
  >  The general shape is worth keeping: a prediction about a row's VALUE presumes the
  >  row RUNS, and on a mutant that kills the guest that presumption is the whole error.

*** THE CHANNEL LINE'S OWN CLAIM, CHECKED A THIRD TIME. ***  Its claim is that a row's
channel-under-its-mutant is knowable at WRITING time, before any table runs.  Here it
held AND it did work the other three checks could not: block 3 proves a disagreeing state
EXISTS, but only the channel line forces the question *what would a read return there?* --
and answering it surfaced the `-1`-outside-the-latch dependency in block 1, which is a
cross-probe coupling nothing else in this round would have named.

====================================================================================
PASS-1 AMENDMENT -- written after the panel answered and STILL BEFORE table 1 ran.
====================================================================================

Four seats changed this design.  Recorded here rather than only in the round block,
because two of them change what the CHANNEL block above claims.

*** 1.  THE ROUND'S OWN §3 NARRATIVE WAS WRONG, AND SO IS THE LEDGER ROW IT CAME FROM.
A PACKET-FED SEAT FOUND IT FROM THE INLINED FACTS ALONE. ***

  The brief said the measured hole is a post-packet push/pop reading `5a,a5` shipped
  versus `ff,ff` swapped, WITH NO COMMAND IN BETWEEN.  The glm seat objected that this
  requires some completion path to clear `atapi_cmd_in_progress`, and named the absence
  of one as a hole in the narrative.

  CHECKED AGAINST THE TREE, and it is right: the flag has exactly two write sites, the
  reset and the packet dispatch.  The completion path sets PHASE_COMPLETED and does not
  touch it.  `memset(d, 0, ...)` exists only in devinit.

  >  SO THE NAIVE FORM OF THIS ROW -- push and pop straight after the transfer -- IS
  >  VACUOUS.  The flag is still 1, the SHIPPED guard refuses too, both legs read ff.
  >
  >  **The RECAL is not scene-setting; it is the only thing that creates a state where
  >  the two builds differ.**  That is a strengthening of the design and a correction to
  >  its stated warrant, which are different things and both are recorded.

  The `wdcatapistswap` ledger row carries the same wrong sentence, inherited from the
  pass-2 seat note that filed it.  It is corrected in place, not silently superseded.

*** 2.  AN OBJECTION THAT WOULD HAVE MADE THE ROW VACUOUS, REFUTED BY AN INVARIANT. ***

  The deepseek seat argued the row is too weak: RECAL leaves `phase == 0`, whereas "the
  real hole state" leaves PHASE_COMPLETED, so a mutant keying on the phase would refuse
  in one and not the other -- and it named `atapi_phase == PHASE_COMPLETED` as a
  one-token escape of all 26 rows.

  MEASURED BY CENSUS, and the state it needs does not exist:

    * `wdc_new_command_reset()` is the ONLY writer of `flag = 0`, and it writes
      `phase = 0` in the same three lines;
    * all five sites writing a nonzero phase (:954 :1256 :1669 :1679 :1713) lie on paths
      where the flag is 1 -- :954 is the line after `flag = 1`, the rest are inside
      ATAPI transfer handling.

  >  **`atapi_cmd_in_progress == 0` IMPLIES `atapi_phase == 0`.**  There is no state with
  >  the flag clear and the phase COMPLETED, so no mutant can use the phase to tell the
  >  post-RECAL state from "the real hole".  RECAL reaches the hole itself.
  >
  >  This invariant is now the NON-VACUITY WARRANT for both M rows and belongs to block 4
  >  above: it is the reason the named channel can vary under E2 and M1 but cannot be
  >  dodged by a phase-keyed edit.

  Its companion claim -- that `atapi_st` might not be set by the time M runs -- is wrong
  on the tree: `atapi_st` is allocated by the PACKET COMMAND dispatch at rows C-I, long
  before the M arm.  The seat is packet-fed and marked the claim REASONED, not READ.

*** 3.  THE CLEARING COMMAND WAS NEARLY THE WRONG ONE. ***

  The minimax seat challenged the brief for inlining the switch without the rest of
  `wdc_command()`.  It has TWO early returns before the switch: a no-disk-image ABRT, and
  a CDROM + WDCC_IDENTIFY ABRT.  This probe attaches a CD image, so IDENTIFY -- the
  natural choice -- would never have reached its case.  RECAL is unaffected, and both
  returns sit AFTER the reset so the flag clears on every path; but the brief had inlined
  an incomplete function, which is the failure the full-enclosing-function rule names.

*** 4.  TWO MECHANICAL CATCHES, BOTH ADOPTED. ***

  * agy: the arm needs `WDCC_RECAL`, the `t7`/`v0` register numbers, AND `REGS` extended
    -- a row naming a register absent from REGS reads `??` and fails.  Verified after
    editing: REGS holds 15 names and every row register is present.  (It said 13 until
    the N arm landed and a measuring seat caught the stale figure -- a count written
    into a durable record two hours before the thing it counts changed under it.)
  * glm: the first draft used `t3` as scratch, and the seat could not tell from the packet
    whether `t3` was read back.  It is not in REGS, so the draft was safe -- but the
    sidestep (do the stores through `t7` and `v0`, the arm's own output registers) costs
    the same eight instructions and removes the question.  Adopted.  The arm now touches
    no register any other arm uses.

  >  NOTE THE ASYMMETRY WORTH KEEPING: glm's concrete claim was FALSE for this probe and
  >  its recommendation was right anyway.  The brief said `t0`-`t6` were "live", using the
  >  word to mean "used by the program" where a reader takes it to mean "read back as a
  >  row".  A seat cannot check REGS from a packet, and the fix was free.

*** 5.  A SECOND VEHICLE WAS ADDED IN-ROUND, AND IT IS THE M5/M6 LESSON ON A NEW AXIS. ***

  The grok seat asked the smallest-edit question of the DESIGN rather than of the shipped
  guard, and found that with only the M arm the hole re-opens under ONE conjunct:

      if (d->atapi_st != NULL && d->atapi_phase != PHASE_CMDOUT &&
          d->cur_command != WDCC_RECAL) {

  After RECAL `cur_command` is 0x10, so the M pushes land and the M rows stay GREEN.
  During DATA-IN and PHASE_COMPLETED `cur_command` is still ATAPI_PKT_CMD, so G/H/I and L
  match the fix.  **After every other no-fill command the writes are refused again** --
  the hole fully re-opened, invisible to a detector with one vehicle.

  >  **A DETECTOR BUILT FROM ONE SCENARIO IS BLIND ALONG THE AXIS THAT SCENARIO HOLDS
  >  FIXED.**  #495 learned this twice in one round -- M5 on the PHASE axis, M6 on the
  >  WIDTH axis -- and the axis here is WHICH NO-FILL OPCODE cleared the flag.  Nobody
  >  looked for a third instance of a lesson two days old until a seat was asked to break
  >  the design rather than to check it.

  FIXED IN-ROUND under L1, not filed: the arm is pre-ship construction, and the repair is
  the same shape as #495's own M6 repair -- add a SECOND no-fill opcode (ATAPI_SOFT_RESET,
  0x08) and read back after each.  One conjunct cannot exempt two opcodes.  Cost: eight
  instructions and two rows.  Both opcodes reach their case bodies on this CD-backed drive
  because neither is IDENTIFY, so neither takes the CDROM early return.

*** 6.  AND THE M7 PREDICTION WAS REFUTED BEFORE THE TABLE RAN -- see the CHANNEL block. ***

  Filed here too because it is a seat finding: a prediction about a row's VALUE presumes
  the row RUNS, and M7 kills the guest at row E.  The accept arms are unreached, not inert.

*** 7.  WHAT THE fable5 SEAT FOUND, BEYOND THE E2 EVIDENCE HOLE ALREADY RECORDED ABOVE. ***

  * The probe's clause-(e) CONTRACT did not name this family.  A CATCHES line was owed and
    is added, plus two BLIND-TO entries for escapes the seat derived (`wdcwidth4` --
    an INEQUALITY width qualifier `&& len <= 2` sweeps everything because no row injects
    at len == 4; and `wdcringresetblind` -- deleting the ring reset escapes every row).
    Both FILED as above-floor coverage debt.
  * The probe header swore "THE PROGRAM AND THE ROWS BELOW ARE UNTOUCHED ... measured 20
    of 20" -- **already false when committed**, because #495 added J/K/L in the same round.
    L4, corrected in place with the instrument's revision history.
  * The wrong post-packet narrative had an uncorrected SIBLING in `OUTSTANDING_BUGS.md`,
    including a REMEDY sentence prescribing the vacuous form of this very row.  Corrected;
    a claim gets fixed everywhere it lives, not only where it was first found.
  * `kill495.sh` computes `WORDS_OK` and **references it nowhere**, while printing "its
    encodings agree -- the run is scoreable".  A check that cannot fail, in the scorer,
    one day after the checkpoint documented that class.  The new driver must wire it in
    or drop the claim; it must not be copied forward as it stands.
  * ACCEPT-ROW FAILABILITY, not floor clause (a), is the rule that governs these rows.
    An accept row is green on both product legs BY DEFINITION, so clause (a) cannot bind
    it; the round's record must say "accept-row failability discharged by a MEASURED
    kill under E2/E3", never "clause (a) satisfied".  As a floor claim it would be
    unfalsifiable, and floor claims are L2-enforceable.

*** 8.  A MEASURING SEAT RAN THE WHOLE TABLE ON REAL BINARIES AND CONFIRMED ALL EIGHT
PREDICTIONS -- THEN FOUND TWO ESCAPES OF THE 15-ROW PROBE, ONE OF THEM A FLOOR BREACH. ***

  Nine mutants, scratch clones only, base-leg apparatus check on all fourteen runs.  Its
  red-counts match this block's predicted arithmetic at EVERY mutant (its 13-row figures
  differ from the ones above by exactly the N rows, which is the arithmetic working).  The
  M7 correction is confirmed the hard way: every register including t7/v0 reads `??`.

  **E4 -- `&& len != 4` SWEEPS 30/30 AND RE-OPENS #495 BYTE-FOR-BYTE.**

      leg                    G     H     I
      base (pre-#495)        a5    5a    00
      shipped fix            c2    c3    c4     <- refused; real chunk-2 disk content
      E4                     a5    5a    00     <- identical to the PRE-FIX build

  The guest is handed its own injected bytes as disk data and the detector scores clean.

  >  **DISPOSITION: L2 FLOOR BREACH, REPAIRED IN-ROUND -- NOT the coverage debt it was
  >  first filed as.**  `if (X)` -> `if (X && Y)` on the predicate #495 ADDED, which
  >  DOMINATES the edited lines, is clause (d)'s canonical form; `#488`'s adjudication
  >  names that exact shape a floor breach.  #495 is closed, so the LATE-DISSENT rule
  >  applies -- disposition 2, measured false pass against a shipped detector's floor,
  >  repaired in a follow-up commit.  **This round is that commit**, and it is already
  >  rewriting the row list, so the repair costs nothing extra.

  THE CLOSURE CONDITION, derived by the seat rather than guessed: `&& P(len)` escapes iff
  P is TRUE on every injected width and FALSE on some IMPLEMENTED width.  {1,2} leaves
  `!= 4`; {2,4} leaves `!= 1`; {1,4} leaves `!= 2`.  **Only {1,2,4} closes it.**

  REPAIRED WITH ONE INSTRUCTION and NO new row: a 4-byte `sw` beside the existing 1-byte
  K injection.  On the fixed leg it is refused like its neighbours (ring empty, row L
  still ST_IDLE); on the base leg it lands like them (DRQ set, still ST_DATAIN).  **It
  kills E4, M3 and M6 together.**  The probe had NO 4-byte emitter at all -- no `sw`, no
  `lw` -- so the device's `case 4:` arm and its `consumed` accounting had never been
  executed on any leg of any run this detector ever made.

  **E5 -- `cur_command == ATAPI_PKT_CMD` SWEEPS 30/30, and the N arm does NOT defeat it.**
  This is a REPLACEMENT of the flag operand, E2's own family, not E3's exemption: after
  both RECAL (0x10) and SOFT_RESET (0x08) `cur_command != 0xa0`, so the guard is false and
  BOTH accept arms stay green.  "One conjunct cannot exempt two opcodes" does not touch it.
  FILED (`wdcflagswap2`), not fixed: its harm class is over-refusal and TRANSIENT, where
  E2's is permanent, and its divergence is derived rather than measured.

  >  **AND THE SEAT NAMED WHAT THE ARM ACTUALLY BUYS, WHICH IS NARROWER THAN THE ROUND
  >  WAS ABOUT TO CLAIM.**  Rows B and M differ in exactly one dimension a mutant can
  >  read -- whether a packet command has ever run.  That is why M1 reds four rows (B and
  >  M, redundantly), and why **E2 is the arm's only UNIQUE kill**: at row B `atapi_st` is
  >  NULL, so E2's guard is false and B stays green.  *"The accept side is pinned" is
  >  measurably false* -- E4 and E5 are both accept-side-relevant and both swept.  The
  >  contract says the narrower thing.

  TWO MORE WRONG RECORDS IT FOUND, both in durable artifacts: `kill495_M6.sh` and
  `kill495_M7.sh` carry M5's header VERBATIM, each claiming the mutant is
  `!= PHASE_CMDOUT -> == PHASE_DATAIN` while their titles and their own Python say
  width-`len==2` and `return 0`.  And it confirmed the `WORDS_OK` dead check independently.

  *It also confirmed the contention hazard from the other side: it saw `killswap.sh`
  appear at 13:09 while its own table was building, and said so.  Holding that table was
  the right call and is now measured rather than assumed.*

